---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const collCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'm1', label: 'mass 1', min: 0.5, max: 10, step: 0.5, value: 2, unit: 'kg' },
    { name: 'm2', label: 'mass 2', min: 0.5, max: 10, step: 0.5, value: 6, unit: 'kg' },
    { name: 'u1', label: 'velocity of 1', min: -10, max: 10, step: 0.5, value: 6, unit: 'm/s' },
    { name: 'u2', label: 'velocity of 2', min: -10, max: 10, step: 0.5, value: 0, unit: 'm/s' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'momentum before (m1u1 + m2u2)', expr: 'm1*u1 + m2*u2', unit: 'kg·m/s' },
    { label: 'velocity of 1 after (elastic)', expr: '((m1 - m2)*u1 + 2*m2*u2)/(m1 + m2)', unit: 'm/s' },
    { label: 'velocity of 2 after (elastic)', expr: '((m2 - m1)*u2 + 2*m1*u1)/(m1 + m2)', unit: 'm/s' },
    { label: 'both stuck together (inelastic)', expr: '(m1*u1 + m2*u2)/(m1 + m2)', unit: 'm/s' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/momentum-and-collisions">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Momentum</strong> — mass times velocity — is the quantity nature refuses to let
    disappear. In every collision, explosion or rocket burn, the total momentum before equals the
    total after. Newton's second law in its original form says force is the <em>rate of change of
    momentum</em>, so with no outside force, the total can't drift. Energy may change form;
    momentum just moves around.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="a 2 kg cart at 6 m/s meets a 6 kg cart at rest — compare the elastic and stuck-together outcomes" widget="sliderLab" config={collCfg} />

  <p>
    Two outcomes bracket reality. <strong>Elastic</strong> (billiard balls): both momentum and
    kinetic energy survive — watch the heavy cart take some speed and the light one bounce back.
    <strong>Inelastic</strong> (clay balls): momentum is still conserved exactly, but kinetic
    energy is lost to crumpling — both carts roll on together at the readout's last value. Make
    the striking mass tiny (m1 = 0.5) and see how little it budges a heavy target.
  </p>

  <h2>Why conservation beats force analysis</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>During a crash you can't know the force.</strong> It spikes unpredictably for milliseconds. Momentum bookkeeping skips the messy middle entirely — before and after suffice.</li>
    <li><strong>Rockets in vacuum:</strong> throw mass backward, gain momentum forward. No ground to push on; conservation is the whole engine.</li>
    <li><strong>Impulse:</strong> force × time = momentum change — why airbags work (same momentum change, spread over longer time, smaller force) and why boxers ride the punch.</li>
    <li><strong>Centre of mass:</strong> in any collision without outside forces, the centre of mass of the system sails through unbothered — the explosion's fragments orbit it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Newton, Principia (1687); OpenStax, College Physics (Momentum and Collisions).</p>
</TopicPage>
